{"name":"Baroness Mauve","hex":"#847098","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"baroness","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The wife of a baron.","A woman holding a baronial title in her own right; a female ruler of a barony."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/baroness"},{"term":"mauve","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rich purple synthetic dye, which faded easily, briefly popular c. 1859‒1873 and now called mauveine.","A pale purple or violet colour, like the colour of the dye after it has faded."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a pale purple colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mauve"}],"entries":[{"title":"Baron","blocks":[{"text":"Baron is a rank of nobility or title of honour, often hereditary, in various European countries, either current or historical. The female equivalent is baroness. Typically, the title denotes an aristocrat who ranks higher than a lord or knight, but lower than a viscount or count. Often, barons hold their fief – their lands and income – directly from the monarch. Barons are less often the vassals of other nobles. In many kingdoms, they were entitled to wear a smaller form of a crown called a coronet."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Heraldic_Crown_of_Spanish_Barons.svg/330px-Heraldic_Crown_of_Spanish_Barons.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baron","wikidata":"Q165503"},{"title":"Mauve","blocks":[{"text":"Mauve is a pale purple color named after the mallow flower. The first use of the word mauve as a color was in 1796–1798 according to the Oxford English Dictionary, but its use seems to have been rare before 1859. Another name for the color is mallow, with the first recorded use of mallow as a color name in English in 1611."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Wilde_Malve.JPG/330px-Wilde_Malve.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mauve","wikidata":"Q604079"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Archive-ugent-be-79D46426-CC9D-11E3-B56B-4FBAD43445F2_DS-0_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Archive-ugent-be-79D46426-CC9D-11E3-B56B-4FBAD43445F2_DS-0_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Archive-ugent-be-79D46426-CC9D-11E3-B56B-4FBAD43445F2_DS-0_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Archive-ugent-be-79D46426-CC9D-11E3-B56B-4FBAD43445F2_DS-0_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A lord of Parliament, also called a baron, illustrated in the manuscript \"Théâtre de tous les peuples et nations de la terre avec leurs habits et ornemens divers, tant anciens que modernes, diligemment depeints au naturel\". Painted by Lucas d'Heere in the second half of the 16th century. Preserved in the Ghent University Library.[4]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AArchive-ugent-be-79D46426-CC9D-11E3-B56B-4FBAD43445F2_DS-0_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Wenceslas_Hollar_-_A_baron_%28State_1%29.jpg/500px-Wenceslas_Hollar_-_A_baron_%28State_1%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Wenceslas_Hollar_-_A_baron_%28State_1%29.jpg/1280px-Wenceslas_Hollar_-_A_baron_%28State_1%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Wenceslas_Hollar_-_A_baron_%28State_1%29.jpg/500px-Wenceslas_Hollar_-_A_baron_%28State_1%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The robe worn by a baron during his creation ceremony in 17th-century Britain, engraved 